; pi4MmuState.pi4 - STAGE 0 of the MMU work. Read the machine's actual
;                   MMU and cache state and print it. Change nothing.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   pmfc pi4MmuState.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns -o mmustate.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS EXISTS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Five files in this tree state what the MMU is doing and they do not
; agree with each other:
;
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib/genet.pi4:318     "THE MMU AND THE CACHES ARE OFF"
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib/memory.pi4:239    "THE MMU IS OFF"
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4:427   "WITH THE MMU AND CACHES ON"
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib/xhci.pi4:275      "The MMU and caches are ON"
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4:376   MMU and caches OFF as kernel8.img
;
; The last three were written when images were entered through U-Boot's
; "go" with U-Boot's page tables live. U-Boot is gone from the stick.
; So two of those five describe a configuration that no longer exists -
; but NOBODY HAS MEASURED WHICH, and a whole staged programme of work
; is about to be built on the answer.
;
; This probe is the measurement. It is free: it reads four system
; registers and prints them, writes nothing, and cannot hang.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THIS IS SAFE, AND HERE IS THE ARGUMENT RATHER THAN THE ASSERTION
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every instruction this probe adds over an empty program is an `mrs`.
; MRS reads. It cannot fault at EL2 for any of the four registers used
; here, it cannot change execution, and it cannot leave the machine in
; a state the monitor will not survive returning to. There is no
; sequence here that a power cycle could be needed for.
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN ANYWAY. Bench practice makes it the rule for EVERY
; payload without exception, and a rule with an exception for "the safe ones" is a rule that gets
; skipped on the day it mattered. It costs one line.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THE OUTPUT MEANS - read this BEFORE running it, not after
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCTLR_EL2 is the line that matters. There is an exact expected value.
;
;   $0000000030C50830  EXPECTED. This is what the firmware's own stub
;                      writes and then erets into us with, quoted at
;                      raspberrypi/tools armstubs/armstub8.S:136-141
;                      (revision 439b6198a9b3), whose comment
;                      reads "All set bits below are res1. LE, no
;                      WXN/I/SA/C/A/M". So this value means the MMU is
;                      OFF, the data cache is OFF, the instruction
;                      cache is OFF, alignment checking is off and
;                      nothing has touched the register since the stub.
;                      genet.pi4 and memory.pi4 are right,
;                      display.pi4:427 and xhci.pi4:275 are stale and
;                      should be corrected in place.
;
;   ANYTHING ELSE      Do NOT read this as "the MMU is on". It means
;                      only that the register is not what the stub on
;                      this disk writes, and there are at least three
;                      quite different reasons for that: the board may
;                      be running a NEWER stub with a different RES1
;                      set; the board may be running the GIC or
;                      high-peripheral variant; or something in our own
;                      startup may be writing SCTLR_EL2. Report the raw
;                      value. Do not start stage 1 until it is
;                      explained - stage 1 rewrites this register, and
;                      rewriting a register whose current value is
;                      unexplained is how a board ends up needing
;                      hands.
;
; CurrentEL should read $8. EL2 is CurrentEL bits 3:2, so EL2 is the
; value 2 in that field, which is $8 in the whole register. The probe
; prints the raw register AND the decoded level side by side on
; purpose: if they disagree, the decode is what is wrong, and printing
; only the decode would hide that.
;
; SCTLR_EL1 is printed because it costs one instruction and it turns a
; puzzle into an answer in the case nobody expects. If this program is
; somehow NOT at EL2, then SCTLR_EL2 is the meaningless read and
; SCTLR_EL1 is the one that governs. Reading both means one bench run
; is conclusive either way instead of needing a second.
;
; HCR_EL2 is printed because it governs whether a stage 2 translation
; regime is in play. If something below us has already established
; stage 2, every plan in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4 changes, and this is
; the cheapest possible place to discover that.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BENCH PROCEDURE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   pmf> deadman 15
;   pmf> map              <- it prints `stage a file at <stage>`. The
;                            --load-addr above does not pin the image.
;   pmf> receive <stage> <len> <crc>
;   pmf> run <stage>
;
; Expected: four labelled hex lines, then the prompt back. The payload
; is built --entry-returns so it returns to the resident monitor rather
; than running off its own end; a payload built without it runs past
; its last instruction, which is why the deadman is armed as well.
;
; WHAT WOULD TELL YOU IT WENT WRONG: no output at all. That would not
; be an MMU finding, it would mean the UART is not where uart.pi4
; thinks it is or the payload never got control, and it is the same
; failure any other diagnostic in this directory would show. Nothing
; this probe does can produce a hang, so a hang is a transfer problem,
; not a result.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PutHex16 - a full 64-bit value, all sixteen digits, always.
;
;  Sixteen digits with no suppression of leading zeroes, deliberately.
;  A system register printed as "30C50830" and a system register
;  printed as "0000000030C50830" carry different information: the
;  second one says the top half was read and was zero, and the first
;  one leaves open the possibility that only 32 bits were fetched. That
;  distinction is exactly the 64-bit trap
;  RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4:1309-1314 records, where an `ldr w9`
;  instead of an `ldr x9` silently loses the top half of an address.
;
;  No character literals - house rule. 48 is the code for the digit
;  zero and 55 is the code for the letter A minus ten, which is the
;  same arithmetic every other probe in this directory uses.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Row - "<label> $<16 hex digits>" and a newline.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Row(*label, v.i)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)                 ; the dollar sign that marks hex here
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define sctlr2.i
  Define entered.i

  UartInit(115200)

  ; Belt and braces over the monitor's deadman. This probe cannot hang,
  ; so this line will never fire; it is here because the habit is the
  ; thing being maintained, not the coverage.
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("pi4MmuState - stage 0, read only")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  Row("CurrentEL raw   ", MmuCurrentEl())
  Row("exception level ", MmuEl())

  sctlr2 = MmuSctlrEl2()
  Row("SCTLR_EL2       ", sctlr2)
  Row("SCTLR_EL2 stub  ", #MMU_SCTLR_EL2_STUB)

  Row("SCTLR_EL1       ", MmuSctlrEl1())
  Row("HCR_EL2         ", MmuHcrEl2())

  UartWriteNl()

  ; The verdict, spelled out, so that the person at the bench does not
  ; have to compare two sixteen-digit numbers by eye at the end of a
  ; long session. The numbers are printed above regardless, because the
  ; verdict is a convenience and the numbers are the finding.
  entered = MmuSctlrEl2Entered()
  If entered = 1
    UartWriteStr("VERDICT: SCTLR_EL2 is exactly what armstub8.S wrote.")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         MMU off, D-cache off, I-cache off. Proven,")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         not decoded - see armstub8.S:136-141.")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("VERDICT: SCTLR_EL2 is NOT the stub value. This does")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         NOT mean the MMU is on. Report the raw")
    UartWriteNl()
    UartWriteStr("         number and do not begin stage 1.")
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()

  UartDrain()

  ; Back to the monitor. Zero is the boring answer and the one the
  ; monitor prints as the payload's return value; the finding is in the
  ; text above, not in this number.
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
